# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: ALFE must close crashed CAD engines and crash-report dialogs on dedicated CI runners
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: native-cad-validation
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-cad-crash-dialogs-and-dedicated-runner-cleanup
#+DATE: 2026-11-02

* Context
The owner confirmed on 2026-11-02 that Windows accounts runner0 through runner3
have no interactive activity: GitLab Runner and successive jobs control them
exclusively. A surviving AutoCAD or BricsCAD in one of these accounts is residue
from an earlier job. This policy does not extend to personal accounts, service
sessions or ALFE attaching to an existing interactive application.

Related existing issues:
- [[file:alfe-cad-owned-com-teardown.issue][Owned COM teardown and restoration acknowledgement]].
- [[file:autocad-com-bootstrap-timeout-leaves-detached-engine.issue][Detached RPC-created AutoCAD after bootstrap failure]].

* Problem
AutoCAD can crash while leaving an Autodesk crash/log submission dialog open.
The job may hang, retain a CAD/COM process or a file handle, and poison the next
job's checkout, profile preparation or license acquisition. BricsCAD failure
and reporting dialogs need equivalent coverage. Ending the bridge or spawned
launcher alone does not establish that a detached COM server or crash reporter
has disappeared.

During SCHMS EPURE qualification, a runner0 AutoCAD COM server survived as
=acad.exe /Automation -Embedding=, parented by RPC rather than the launched
engine. The owner confirmed it was a disposable test session and authorized
its closure. Subsequent jobs failed before tests while cleaning locked files:
[[https://fabrik.sncf.fr/gitlab/dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms/-/jobs/564880][AutoCAD 564880]] and
[[https://fabrik.sncf.fr/gitlab/dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms/-/jobs/564881][BricsCAD 564881]].
This incident confirms residual processes/locks; no crash-report dialog was
observed in it. Crash/log submission dialogs are a separately reported failure
scenario to reproduce, not an inferred cause of those two jobs.

* Proposal
- Make ALFE teardown bounded on success, Lisp error, bootstrap timeout, CAD
  crash, cancellation and bridge failure; use a supervisor that survives bridge
  failure and records the exact engine/COM identities before blocking calls.
- Restore live session settings when possible, then close the application and
  only its owned descendants/reporters. Detect Autodesk crash/log reporting
  dialogs and dismiss or terminate the owned reporter without submitting logs.
  Preserve diagnostics first; never convert a crash into a successful test.
- Cover RPC-parented COM servers, not merely the launcher's descendant tree.
  Use real Windows owner SID, PID plus creation time and invocation receipts;
  inherited USERNAME may be stale. Wait for PID disappearance and file release.
- In explicitly configured dedicated CI accounts, preflight removes residual
  CAD engines/reporters owned by that exact account before checkout and launch.
  Keep this broader account policy separate from ordinary ALFE attachment:
  an attached interactive instance must remain untouched.
- Archive crash/bridge logs, dialog diagnostics and cleanup receipts as job
  artifacts. Report incomplete cleanup explicitly and fail the job.

* Acceptance criteria
1. Deterministic Windows fixtures cover a hung crash-report dialog, a reporter
   surviving its CAD parent, detached COM activation, timeout, cancellation,
   normal exit and an inherited USERNAME different from the real identity.
2. All processes owned by the invocation disappear within a fixed budget and
   checkout drawing logs can be opened exclusively or removed afterward.
3. No Autodesk log submission occurs; the original CAD failure stays visible
   and the job exits nonzero with diagnostic artifacts.
4. Dedicated-account preflight recovers from a previous job's residue; a second
   job on the same runner completes checkout and launches AutoCAD/BricsCAD.
5. A foreign account, service session and pre-existing interactive attachment
   remain untouched. Ownership tests use SID and process creation identity.
6. Native Windows qualification covers both AutoCAD and BricsCAD. Do not close
   this issue on fixture-only or successful-shutdown-only evidence.
